    "content": "Completes the public recovery path, preserves command and delivered flow as distinct objects, and names the author or manufacturer receipts that would reopen numerical scoring. UD Lehigh/Bitlismen Pump-Flow Recovery Status v0.2 Generated: 2026-09-09T01:28:51.649839+00:00 Status: PUBLIC_SOURCE_EXHAUSTED / AUTHOR_OR_MANUFACTURER_RECEIPT_REQUIRED Claim class: SOURCE_RECOVERY_AUDIT Physical promotion: 0 1. Controlling UD theorem For the split-invariant subchannel, G14=Δq1−Δq24,G23=−G14. G_{14} = \\frac{\\Delta q_1-\\Delta q_2}{4}, \\qquad G_{23}=-G_{14}. The unknown M1/M2 valve split parameters are not load-bearing for this subchannel. The only remaining Lehigh numeric source requirement is therefore: Δq1,Δq2 \\Delta q_1,\\Delta q_2 as source-authenticated delivered pump-flow changes. 2. Public Lehigh source findings The 2026 physical-QTP paper describes the Lehigh laboratory apparatus as: four interconnected tanks; two VFD pumps; pressure sensors for tank-level measurement; SCADA/LabVIEW/MATLAB monitoring and control; full measurement of all four tank levels. Its physical validation plots label the actuator traces as: Pump 1 speed command; Pump 2 speed command. The paper explicitly discusses the nonlinear relation between pump speed and delivered flow. Its Data Availability statement is: Data will be made available on request. No source-authored synchronized delivered-flow channel or Lehigh-specific pump-command/speed-to-flow calibration was recovered from the public article/package. 3. Manufacturer findings Bitlismen identifies the April 2024 Lehigh installation as a custom Quadruple Tank Process Control Research Platform. The public product description provides: a pressure sensor on each tank; two independently controlled pumps; programmatic flow control; solenoid control of pump inlet flow; open-source LabVIEW-oriented software. The public product page does not expose a Lehigh-specific pump calibration table or identify a direct pump-flow sensor chan