e channels share gain, reference, DAQ, temperature, clock, or other correlated error. 4. Full first-order covariance law — uncertain tank areas The preregistration correctly requires tank-area tolerance if material. Define z=(A1,A2,A3,A4,Δm1,Δm2,Δm3,Δm4,ΔfL,ΔfR)T. z = ( A_1,A_2,A_3,A_4, \\Delta m_1,\\Delta m_2,\\Delta m_3,\\Delta m_4, \\Delta f_L,\\Delta f_R )^T. The exact residual is bilinear in Ai,ΔmiA_i,\\Delta m_i. Its first-order sensitivity row is JR=14(Δm1,−Δm2,−Δm3,Δm4,A1,−A2,−A3,A4,−1,1). \\boxed{ J_R = \\frac14 ( \\Delta m_1,-\\Delta m_2,-\\Delta m_3,\\Delta m_4, A_1,-A_2,-A_3,A_4, -1,1 ). } Therefore the general GUM-style first-order propagation is u2(R14)≈JRΣzJRT. \\boxed{ u^2(R_{14}) \\approx J_R\\,\\Sigma_z\\,J_R^T. } This is the governing standard-uncertainty law when area uncertainty is non-negligible. It preserves: area-area covariance; slope-slope covariance; flow-flow covariance; area-slope covariance; level/flow common DAQ covariance if source-authored; cross-state calibration covariance. No covariance may be assigned numerically by convenience. 5. Independent-input specialization If, and only if, the authenticated calibration receipt justifies independence of: the four tank-area errors; the four slope-change errors; the two flow-change errors; then the first-order law reduces to u2(R14)≈116[∑i=14(Ai2u2(Δmi)+(Δmi)2u2(Ai))+u2(ΔfL)+u2(ΔfR)]. \\boxed{ u^2(R_{14}) \\approx \\frac1{16} \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^4 \\left( A_i^2u^2(\\Delta m_i) + (\\Delta m_i)^2u^2(A_i) \\right) + u^2(\\Delta f_L) + u^2(\\Delta f_R) \\right]. } For statistically independent random variables AiA_i and Δmi\\Delta m_i, the exact product-variance correction is Var(AiΔmi)=Ai2u2(Δmi)+(Δmi)2u2(Ai)+u2(Ai)u2(Δmi). {\\mathrm Var}(A_i\\Delta m_i) = A_i^2u^2(\\Delta m_i) + (\\Delta m_i)^2u^2(A_i) + u^2(A_i)u^2(\\Delta m_i). Thus the exact independent-product specialization is u2(R14)=116[∑i=14(Ai2u2(Δmi)+(Δmi)2u2(Ai)+u2(Ai)u2(Δmi))+u2(ΔfL)+u2(ΔfR)] \\boxed{ u^2(R_{14}) = \\frac1{16} \\le