    "content": "Locates a public thesis and narrows the exact institutional and author-held raw-data targets without treating methodological precedent as an executable corpus. ST3S Institutional Archive / Thesis Second-Pass Search v0.1 Date: 2026-09-10 Scope: university repositories, thesis deposits, library records, institutional research portals, institutional data portals and author-hosted academic deposits associated with the two brightest near-miss lineages. Verdict: INSTITUTIONAL_ARCHIVE SOURCE-AVAILABILITY NO-GO Physical promotion: 0 Executive result The second-pass archive hunt did not locate an openly accessible raw corpus that can execute the frozen ST3S v0.1A scorer. It did identify one important institutional artifact: Fabio Ansaloni’s complete 2020 University of Copenhagen PhD thesis is openly deposited and contains extensive time-domain acquisition details for the same foundry-fabricated quantum-dot program. That thesis materially narrows the data request but is not itself a raw event corpus. For the Broome/Gorman lineage: Sam Gorman’s 2019 UNSW PhD thesis title is independently confirmed as “Charge and spin dynamics in multi-donor systems”; the UNSW researcher/publication portals expose the 2016 real-time triple-dot paper; targeted searches did not surface a public raw-data record, thesis file or data deposit tied to the 2016 experiment; UNSWWorks itself blocks crawler access, so this search cannot assert that no inaccessible or unindexed institutional record exists. The scientific result remains source availability, not theory failure. 1. Ansaloni / Kuemmeth lineage 1.1 Institutional thesis deposit — FOUND The University of Copenhagen Research Portal hosts the thesis: Fabio Ansaloni, Single-electron control in one- and two-dimensional arrays of silicon quantum dots, PhD thesis, Niels Bohr Institute, 2020. The institutional portal links: a 91.1 MB final thesis file; the Royal Danish Library catalogue record. No separate dataset is listed on the thesis research-output page. 1.2 Time-domain instrumentation documented in the thesis The thesis contains a dedicated Time domain analysis section. It documents, among other measurements: fast charge acquisition using an Alazar 9360 digitizer; a 1 MSa/s time-domain charge measurement in one experiment; continuously recorded sensor response digitized at 500 kS/s with 11 µs integration in the triple/double-dot control study; single-shot charge traces; controlled electron shuttling within the two-dimensional dot array; triple-dot tomography and high-dimensional charge-state acquisition. These facts confirm that the laboratory had instrumentation capable of producing raw time-domain traces relevant to a future ST3S acquisition. 1.3 Why the thesis does not close the corpus gap The deposited thesis contains figures, analysis, descriptions and aggregate results. The institutional research-output record does not expose an accompanying raw trace dataset. The Royal Danish Library link is a catalogue/thesis-access record, not a public experimental-data deposit. Therefore: ANSALONI_THESIS = METHOD_AND_ACQUISITION_PROVENANCE not ST3S_RAW_CORPUS. 1.4 Exact archive targets now identifiable A future data request can be much more specific. Highest-value requested blocks are: native sensor-voltage time traces underlying thesis Sec. 5.3.1 time-domain measurements; the data underlying Fig. 5.3.2 or equivalent 500 kS/s charge traces; raw single-shot sensor traces from Sec. 5.3.2; any unaveraged dwell/tunneling blocks in which the source evolves without a forced terminal bit pattern; raw gate/sensor time series behind Chapter 6 electron-shuttling acquisitions; pulse metadata, digitizer clock, sensor calibration and physical dot labels; if available, any passive/unbiased dwell period preceding a programmed pulse. Programmed shuttling remains a controlled-source lane. It cannot substitute for a natural-source ST3S block. 2. Broome / Gorman lineage 2.1 Public institutional identity — CONFIRMED UNSW’s current staff/research pages identify Dr Sam Gorman and list: Broome et al., “Mapping the Chemical Potential Landscape of a Triple Quantum Dot,” Phys. Rev. B 94, 054314 (2016). The paper itself studies non-equilibrium charge dynamics using real-time charge sensing and reconstructs tunneling paths of individual electrons. This remains the strongest natural-source semantic near-match. 2.2 Gorman PhD thesis — TITLE CONFIRMED, PUBLIC FILE NOT LOCATED UNSW and the Australian Institute of Physics independently identify Gorman’s 2019 award-winning thesis as: “Charge and spin dynamics in multi-donor systems.” Targeted searches for the exact title across public web indexes, Australian thesis/catalogue domains and UNSW-facing search results did not surface a directly accessible thesis PDF or data attachment. UNSWWorks blocked direct crawler access during this audit. Therefore the correct typed result is: THESIS_EXISTS / PUBLIC_FILE_NOT_RESOLVED_BY_THIS_SEARCH not THESIS_NOT_PUBLIC. 2.3 Institutional raw-data search Targeted searches across: UNSW researcher profiles; UNSW public research pages; Australian research-data indexing; UNSW/figshare-style domain variants; exact paper title + dataset/data/supplement queries; exact thesis title + repository/data queries did not surface a public raw trace corpus for the 2016 triple-dot experiment. The publicly indexed artifacts remain the paper/preprint and author publication records. 2.4 Exact acquisition target The ideal request is not “all data.” It is: complete native SET/charge-sensor time traces underlying the non-equilibrium tunneling-path measurements in the 2016 triple-dot paper; physical charge-state labels for each distinguishable triple-dot coordinate/state; time stamps/sample rate; gate-voltage and reservoir-bias metadata; calibration traces used to threshold sensor states; complete contiguous acquisition blocks, not target-selected examples; any passive dwell or spontaneous-tunneling segments before/without a forced destination; event ordering sufficient to construct a held-out block with N−2G≥600N-2G\\ge600 after a calibration-frozen guard. This would directly test whether the Broome/Gorman archive can satisfy ST3S v0.1A. 3. Archive search matrix University of Copenhagen / NBI FOUND - institutional PhD thesis page; - full thesis PDF; - Royal Danish Library catalogue record; - detailed time-domain acquisition methodology. NOT FOUND - linked dataset record for the thesis; - public native trace archive for the 500 kS/s measurements; - institutional dataset page tied to the specific thesis experiment. UNSW / Broome-Gorman FOUND - current institutional staff/research pages; - publication record; - thesis title confirmation; - real-time triple-dot paper/preprint. NOT RESOLVED - public thesis file for Gorman from the indexed search; - public raw triple-dot trace deposit; - public data DOI/Zenodo/Figshare/ResearchData record tied to the 2016 experiment. ACCESS LIMIT - UNSWWorks direct crawling was blocked by robots.txt. 4. Scientific disposition EMPIRICAL - Ansaloni’s institutional thesis confirms relevant high-bandwidth time-domain acquisition existed. - Broome/Gorman’s experiment confirms real-time natural/non-equilibrium triple-dot charge dynamics. NO-GO - no qualifying institutional raw-data deposit was located for either lineage. SOURCE-BLOCKED - actual native traces remain unavailable from the public archive path searched. OPEN - inaccessible/unindexed institutional deposits; - author-held raw data; - lab data servers or archival media; - manually navigated UNSWWorks records not exposed to crawler search. No v0.1A empirical score was run. Physical promotion remains 0."
